Step 1 — Understand the complete system









1. Arduino Uno
The brain of the car. Runs the code and controls the motors, servo and sensor.












2. L298N motor driver
Controls the direction and speed of the four DC motors through two motor channels.












3. Ultrasonic sensor
Measures the distance between the car and a nearby object, such as a student's hand.












4. Servo motor
Rotates the ultrasonic sensor to look left and right.



The four wheels should be grouped into left-side motors and right-side motors. The L298N has two motor channels, so each channel controls one side of the car.
Step 2 — Connect the four wheel motors
First, disconnect the battery and USB cable.







forum.arduino.cc


The L298N has four motor output terminals, grouped into two channels.
L298N terminal	Connect to
OUT1 and OUT2	Both left-side motors, connected in parallel
OUT3 and OUT4	Both right-side motors, connected in parallel
For each side, connect the two motors so that they rotate in the same direction when commanded forward. If one wheel rotates backward, reverse that motor's two wires.
Important: The L298N's current capacity must be sufficient for both motors on each channel. Four motors can draw substantial current, especially when the wheels are blocked. If the motors exceed the driver's limits, use a more suitable driver.
Step 3 — Connect the L298N to the Arduino Uno
Use the following table exactly for this build.
L298N pin	Arduino Uno
ENA	D5
IN1	D8
IN2	D9
IN3	D10
IN4	D11
ENB	D6
GND	GND
For speed control, remove the ENA and ENB jumpers if fitted, and connect the enable pins to D5 and D6 as shown. This lets the code control motor speed using PWM. 

